**Change the write and recovery protocol first.** As designed, `dst` can contain stale or partial bytes while the manifest says the file is synced.

| Priority | What breaks | Decision to change |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Copy integrity | A same-size edit within one second is invisible (`state.py:19`). Writing directly to `dst` exposes partial files, and `syncd.py:52–61` journals success even when the post-copy check fails. | Stream to a temporary file beside the target, validate it, atomically replace the target, **then** journal success. Use nanosecond timestamps as a fast filter and content comparison when equality matters. |
| 2. Locking and recovery | Two processes can both pass the PID-file check. Releasing the lock before commit lets an older whole-manifest write overwrite a newer one. A torn manifest fails JSON parsing, and startup never calls journal replay. | Hold one OS-backed lock per destination for the daemon’s lifetime, starting before recovery. Replay on startup, tolerate a torn final journal record, and compact only after an atomic, durable manifest checkpoint. |
| 3. Deletion | The current loop never removes vanished paths. Adding deletion by scan difference alone turns an unreadable source directory into apparent mass deletion. A file vanishing during copy can also stop the daemon. | Distinguish **confirmed absent** from **could not scan**. Delete only managed paths after a successful scan of their subtree; handle disappearance during copy per file. |
| 4. Latency and backlog | A 200 ms timer checked only at 500 ms polls costs another poll even for a quiet file. A continuously changing file can wait forever. A dict coalesces changes to one path; it does not bound the number of dirty paths or the time to copy them. | Drop the 700 ms guarantee. Define latency from the **last edit**, set a copy budget per cycle, and measure the age of the oldest pending change. |
| 5. Polling cost | At the proposed cadence, 50,000 files imply roughly **100,000 stats/sec** before copying. The loop also parses and rewrites the full manifest every idle cycle, then sleeps 500 ms *after* the work. | Keep the manifest in memory and checkpoint only changes. Benchmark whole-cycle p95/p99 on cold caches and during builds before fixing the poll interval or ruling out watchers. |
